# Exercise: Reading an Argument
#
# `args.at` retrieves a single argument by index:
#
#   args.at  ( Int -- String )
#
# What happens if the index is out of range? `args.at` returns the
# *empty string*, not a panic. That makes it forgiving but a little
# ambiguous — you can't immediately tell "argument not provided"
# apart from "argument was an empty string". For most CLI tools
# that distinction doesn't matter; when it does, you bounds-check
# with `args.count` first (next exercise).
#
# Your task: Write `arg-empty?` — given an index, return true if
# the argument at that index is the empty string (either because
# it really is empty *or* because it's out of bounds).
#
# Use `string.empty?` from chapter 13 to make the test.
#
# ## To see it on a real command line
#
# Once you've solved it:
#
#     ─── try-at.seq ────────────────────────────────
#     include "exercises/21-args/02-at"
#
#     : main ( -- Int )
#         1 arg-empty?
#         [ "arg 1 missing or empty" ]
#         [ "arg 1 present"          ]
#         if io.write-line
#         0
#     ;
#     ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#     $ seqc build try-at.seq -o try-at
#     $ ./try-at                 → arg 1 missing or empty
#     $ ./try-at hello           → arg 1 present
#     $ ./try-at ""              → arg 1 missing or empty
#
# That last case is the "missing vs explicitly empty" ambiguity —
# both produce the same answer here. The next exercise builds the
# bounds-check that lets you tell them apart.
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: arg-empty? ( Int -- Bool )
    # args.at the index, then string.empty?
    drop true
;

: test-program-path-not-empty ( -- )
    # 0 args.at is the program path itself — never empty.
    0 arg-empty?
    # Do not edit below this line
    not test.assert
;

: test-out-of-bounds-is-empty ( -- )
    # Index 99 is way past the end, so args.at returns "".
    99 arg-empty?
    # Do not edit below this line
    test.assert
;
